\title{bbqr: Bayesian Quantile Regression with Lasso and Adaptive Lasso}
\description{
Markov chain Monte Carlo samplers for Bayesian quantile regression, based on the asymmetric Laplace distribution and the location-scale mixture representation of Kozumi and Kobayashi (2011) \doi{10.1080/00949655.2010.496117}. A binary response and an observed continuous response are both supported, each with three penalty layers behind one interface: no penalty, following Benoit and Van den Poel (2012) \doi{10.1002/jae.1216}; the Bayesian lasso, following Benoit, Al-Hamzawi and Yu (2013) \doi{10.1007/s00180-013-0407-8}; and the Bayesian adaptive lasso of Rubio Garcia (2023) \url{https://soar.wichita.edu/entities/publication/a2f86232-4704-4ec2-b685-751e7b04ec42}. In the binary family each is available as published and in a corrected form, the default, in which every improper prior component is replaced by a proper one so that the posterior exists unconditionally; the continuous family ships the corrected form only. The continuous adaptive-lasso layer at its default reproduces the penalty of Alhamzawi, Yu and Benoit (2012) \doi{10.1177/1471082X1101200304}. A binary threshold model identifies the coefficient vector only up to a positive scale, so the binary samplers expose the identification anchor as an explicit argument, allowing fixing the scale of the error distribution, fixing a single coefficient, and constraining the norm of the coefficient vector to be compared directly; an observed response identifies the scale, so the continuous samplers have no anchor and draw it every sweep. The MCMC cores are written in Fortran and called from R.
}
\section{Two response families}{

The package fits the same three penalty layers to two kinds of response,
through two parallel entry points.

\describe{
\item{\code{\link[=bbqr]{bbqr()}}}{A \strong{binary} response, observed only as 0/1 through a
threshold. The latent response is drawn each sweep. Because the
threshold identifies the coefficient vector only up to a positive
scale, these fits take an \code{anchor} that says how the scale is fixed.
\code{bbqr_alasso()}, \code{bbqr_lasso()} and \code{bbqr_none()} are the direct forms.}
\item{\code{\link[=cbqr]{cbqr()}}}{An \strong{observed continuous} response. There is no latent
\emph{response} draw and no \code{anchor}: the data identify the scale, so \code{sigma}
is sampled every sweep. The location-scale latents \code{z} and \code{s} are still
drawn. These fits instead take \code{q}, the exponent on
\code{sigma} in the local-scale prior rate, which the binary model cannot
see because its \code{sigma} is held fixed. \code{cbqr_alasso()}, \code{cbqr_lasso()}
and \code{cbqr_none()} are the direct forms.}
}

The two families do \strong{not} share a prior constructor. \code{\link[=prior]{prior()}} builds a
\code{bbqr.prior} object for the binary fitters; the continuous fitters take a
plain named list, because their hierarchy has different components (no
anchor, a live \code{sigma}, and the \code{q} exponent) and different reference
defaults. Passing a \code{\link[=prior]{prior()}} object to \code{cbqr()} is an error, and says so.
}

\section{Method sources}{

The binary samplers implement Rubio Garcia (2023) for the adaptive lasso,
and Benoit, Al-Hamzawi and Yu (2013) and Benoit and Van den Poel (2012) for
the lasso and unpenalised layers. On the continuous side the published
specification each layer reproduces is a different one: at its default
\code{q = 1} the adaptive lasso is the penalty of Alhamzawi, Yu and Benoit
(2012), the lasso at \code{q = 0} is Benoit, Al-Hamzawi and Yu (2013) carried
over to an observed response, and the unpenalised layer is the sampler of
Kozumi and Kobayashi (2011). See \code{citation("bbqr")}.

\strong{In the binary family}, each of the three penalties is shipped in two
forms, selected by \code{prior(penalty, model = )}: \code{"v3"}, the hierarchy as
published, and \code{"v5"}, the default, in which every improper prior component
has been replaced by a proper one so that the posterior exists
unconditionally. The published adaptive-lasso and lasso hierarchies have no
posterior, for the reason given in \code{\link[=prior]{prior()}}; they are kept so that earlier
results can be reproduced, and the print method labels them. The continuous
family ships one hierarchy, the proper one: there is no \code{model} argument and
no published continuous specification being reproduced alongside it.
}

\section{Getting started}{

\code{\link[=bbqr]{bbqr()}} is the entry point for a 0/1 response; see its help page for the
three penalties, the identification anchors and the derivation versions.
\code{\link[=cbqr]{cbqr()}} is the entry point for a continuous one. \code{\link[=prior]{prior()}} sets
hyperparameters and selects the hierarchy for the binary fitters only; the
continuous fitters take a plain named list, documented at \code{\link[=cbqr]{cbqr()}}.
}

\section{Relationship to bayesQR}{

The Fortran RNG wrapper (\code{src/wrapper.c}) and the general layout of the
package follow Benoit's \pkg{bayesQR}, which is GPL (>= 2) licensed and is
acknowledged in the \code{Authors@R} field of \code{DESCRIPTION}. The six MCMC
kernels shipped here -- three binary, three continuous -- were written for
this package.
